WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.314
Prefix sums and Fenwick trees answer one question very fast: what is the total between position l and position r?

00:00:08.664 --> 00:00:10.162
Think of a car odometer.

00:00:10.612 --> 00:00:15.442
To know how far you drove between Tuesday and Friday, you do not re-drive the road.

00:00:15.892 --> 00:00:19.677
You read the odometer on Friday and subtract the reading from Tuesday.

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:25.804
A prefix sum array is an odometer for an array: each cell stores the running total so far.

00:00:27.154 --> 00:00:29.418
Here is an array and its prefix array.

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:34.790
P of i is the sum of the first i numbers, with P of zero equal to zero.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:39.733
The sum from index one to index three is P of four minus P of one.

00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:44.677
That is twelve minus three, which is nine, computed with one subtraction.

00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:50.067
Prefix sums plus a hash map count subarrays that add up to k.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:55.114
As we walk, we keep a map from each prefix total to how many times we saw it.

00:00:55.564 --> 00:01:02.217
If the current total minus k was seen before, every earlier spot with that total starts a subarray summing to k.

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.616
This turns an n squared search into one pass.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:13.409
But plain prefix sums break when values change: one update forces you to rewrite every later total.

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:18.260
A Fenwick tree, also called a binary indexed tree, fixes this.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.946
Each cell stores the sum of a block whose length is the lowest set bit of its index.

00:01:24.396 --> 00:01:29.945
Update and query both walk about log n cells, by adding or removing that lowest bit.

00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:33.838
Here is a query for the first seven numbers.

00:01:34.288 --> 00:01:38.212
Seven is binary one one one. Cell seven covers one number.

00:01:38.662 --> 00:01:42.203
Drop the lowest bit to get six, which covers two numbers.

00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:47.320
Drop again to get four, which covers four numbers. Three cells, and we are done.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:57.285
Static data: use a prefix array. Changing data: use a Fenwick tree. Ranges of any shape: say segment tree as the follow up.
